Yes. I went back through the Pantheon history and treated repetition as multiple renderings of the same underlying machinery, rather than deleting it as redundancy. I also checked the architecture against adjacent work in open-ended evolution, novelty search, concept formation, and self-improving agents; there is real neighboring research, which makes the Pantheon stronger when we specify exactly what its integration contributes rather than overclaiming novelty. Novelty Search, for example, demonstrates why direct objective optimization can be deceptive, while newer open-ended work explicitly studies transformational search spaces and empirically validated self-improvement. 

